WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.200
Why does this matter?

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.713
Some workloads can't leave Kerberos yet.

3
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:06.655
A traditional service
account with an S P N and a

4
00:00:06.655 --> 00:00:09.900
password that never expires
is the riskiest of them.

5
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.885
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
group managed service accounts says

6
00:00:14.885 --> 00:00:19.325
a G M S A gives the same workload a
password that Windows changes for it.

7
00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:24.833
And Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts says a D M S A, on a

8
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:30.875
Windows Server 2025 domain, supersedes
the old account and disables its password.

9
00:00:30.875 --> 00:00:35.185
But a G M S A works only while
Active Directory is authoritative,

10
00:00:35.185 --> 00:00:39.412
which is why the program's guidance
gives every one an end date.

11
00:00:39.412 --> 00:00:41.338
So converting isn't the end.

12
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:46.935
Every G M S A and D M S A must either
move to a workload identity, retire,

13
00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:52.737
or sit on a dated Kerberos island list,
with an S P N owner and an end date.

14
00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:54.827
In this module you plan all three,

15
00:00:54.827 --> 00:00:58.440
and you see the write path
refuse a plan nobody approved.
